<!--
  ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
  ║  ONE-SHOT DESTRUCTIVE CHANGES  (use only when all components are listed) ║
  ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

  This file deletes the ENTIRE dependency chain in a single deployment.
  Salesforce processes these atomically, so listing every component that
  references the deleted metadata avoids compilation failures.

  ⚠  WHEN THIS APPROACH IS SAFE
  ────────────────────────────────
  It works ONLY when every component that references the deleted metadata is
  ALSO listed here. If even one referencing component is omitted (e.g. a test
  class that queries OrderSummary__c, or a Flow that calls OrderController),
  the deploy will fail with a compilation error.

  ✅ PREFERRED APPROACH FOR JENKINS
  ────────────────────────────────────
  Use destructiveChangesPre.xml + destructiveChangesPost.xml instead. That
  approach makes dependencies explicit and is easier to debug when a stage
  fails mid-pipeline.

  Jenkins command for this one-shot file:
    sf deploy metadata \
      --manifest manifest/package.xml \
      --post-destructive-changes manifest/destructiveChanges.xml

  Dependency chain being removed:
    orderDashboard (LWC)
      → OrderController → OrderService
        → OrderSummary__c.Status__c → OrderSummary__c
-->